// Live online Cambridge IGCSE, A-Level, Pearson Edexcel, IB Diploma,
// and American AP for expat, tech-sector, and Finnish families across
// Helsinki, Espoo, Tampere, Turku, Oulu, and Rovaniemi/Lapland.
// COMPLETES THE NORDIC SET (Denmark, Sweden, Norway, Finland).
//
// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — READ BEFORE EDITING:
// Finland is a CONFIDENT, PERMISSIVE market (notification tier) with
// two honest nuances that must appear wherever the framework is
// described. The paradox is the story: the country with the world's
// most admired school system also fully trusts parents.
// BASIC EDUCATION PHASE (perusopetus, roughly ages 7-16):
// - Kotiopetus requires NO permission from any authority — the
//   guardian's NOTIFICATION (ilmoitus) to the municipality of
//   residence suffices, and the OPH (Finnish National Agency for
//   Education) states explicitly that the education provider CANNOT
//   forbid it. The guardian decides.
// - The child leaves the school roll entirely (erotodistus issued) —
//   unlike Hungary's enrolled model. From notification, the guardian
//   is responsible for the oppivelvollisuus being completed; neglect
//   can bring a guardian fine (sakko).
// - MONITORING: the municipality must monitor progress; standard
//   practice is a designated TUTKIVA OPETTAJA (examining teacher)
//   who follows progress against the age-appropriate objectives of
//   the national core curriculum and gives a WRITTEN YEAR-END REPORT.
//   Home-educated students are not graded numerically. Municipal
//   practice varies.
// - NUANCE ONE — LANGUAGE: teaching in a foreign language is
//   expressly permitted — but a permanently resident child must,
//   within compulsory-education age, attain knowledge and skills
//   corresponding to the FINNISH perusopetus syllabus tied to the
//   national core curriculum. English-medium Cambridge is fine; the
//   Finnish-syllabus progression must be demonstrable to the tutkiva
//   opettaja. A soft dual-track — plan it, and say so.
// - No free materials, school meals, student welfare services, or
//   money; the municipality has no duty to arrange teaching.
// - NUANCE TWO — CERTIFICATES: no ordinary paattotodistus. The
//   ERITYINEN TUTKINTO (special examination) route exists for a
//   certificate of the basic syllabus (whole or part) — but
//   municipalities are NOT obliged to arrange it, so agree early
//   with the tutkiva opettaja / local principal. Fallback: lukio and
//   vocational admission via discretionary selection
//   (harkinnanvarainen valinta) without the certificate.
// THE 2021 EXTENSION — STATE CAREFULLY, IT IS THE NORDIC TWIST:
// Since August 2021 oppivelvollisuus runs to AGE 18 (or earlier
// completion of an upper-secondary qualification — ylioppilastutkinto
// or vocational — OR "EQUIVALENT FOREIGN EDUCATION", the law's own
// words). Finland therefore has NO Sweden/Norway-style free post-16
// window — NEVER claim one. But the OPH is equally explicit that the
// obligation CAN be completed WITHOUT participating in teaching:
// via the lukio erityinen tutkinto or direct vocational competence
// demonstrations (nayttoja), with the young person acquiring the
// competence independently; and the municipality of residence holds
// guidance/supervision responsibility for home-educated youths'
// transition (Oppivelvollisuuslaki § 11). Whether a given
// international programme counts as "equivalent foreign education"
// is assessed case by case — refer families to the municipality's
// guidance services; never assert the outcome.
// MARKET NOTE: Finland's international schools are largely PUBLIC —
// free or modest, but small, city-bound, and oversubscribed. The
// gate is CAPACITY, not fees (ISH Helsinki is the private
// exception). And the Cambridge track barely exists in the country:
// the market is IB + Finnish national. CAIE exam sittings are thin —
// hedge exam logistics; Baltic/Nordic capitals (Tallinn two hours by
// ferry from Helsinki) are practical alternatives, confirmed per
// session. Do not name unverified centres.
// TIMEZONE — THE BEST ALIGNMENT IN OUR COVERAGE: Finland runs EET
// (UTC+2) / EEST (UTC+3 summer). Nairobi EAT is UTC+3 — so Finland
// is 0-1 hours behind, and IN SUMMER HELSINKI AND NAIROBI SHARE THE
// SAME CLOCK. Use this framing, not the CET one.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

