  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Finland?', a: 'Yes — and no permission is needed: the guardian\'s notification to the municipality suffices, the school cannot forbid it, and the child leaves the roll entirely. The municipality monitors progress through a designated examining teacher against the national core curriculum, with a written year-end report and no numeric grading.' },
    { q: 'Can we home educate in English?', a: 'Yes — expressly permitted. The honest nuance: a permanently resident child must still attain knowledge corresponding to the Finnish basic-education syllabus within compulsory-education age, demonstrable to the examining teacher. We plan that Finnish-syllabus progression into the year deliberately.' },
    { q: 'What does the examining teacher actually do?', a: 'Follows the child\'s progress against the national core curriculum\'s age objectives — through discussions, work samples, or written exercises, varying by municipality — and writes a year-end report. We treat the relationship as a partnership and supply documentation continuously, exactly as our Norway families do with the tilsyn.' },
    { q: 'How do certificates work without a school?', a: 'There is no ordinary leaving certificate. The erityinen tutkinto (special examination) provides one for families who want it — agreed early, since municipalities are not obliged to arrange it — and upper-secondary admission through discretionary selection works without it, where an externally examined Cambridge record is the strongest possible file.' },
    { q: 'Is there a free window after 16 like Sweden and Norway?', a: 'No — Finland extended compulsory education to 18 in 2021, and we will not invent a window. But the obligation ends early on an upper-secondary qualification or equivalent foreign education, and it can be completed without attending teaching (the lukio special examination, vocational demonstrations). How a given international programme is treated is confirmed with the municipality\'s guidance services — a conversation we plan with every family before sixteen.' },
    { q: 'Do home-educating families get the famous free materials and meals?', a: 'No — those attach to school attendance. Home-educating families bear their own costs, and no allowance is paid. What the framework gives instead is complete freedom of method under a light monitoring relationship.' },
    { q: 'Why choose Cambridge in an IB-and-Finnish country?', a: 'Because the Cambridge track has no local carrier at all — and for UK-bound students it is the direct UCAS route, while Finnish universities admit A-Levels through Studyinfo, keeping the domestic door open beside it.' },
    { q: 'Where do Finland students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At authorised centres confirmed per family per session — Finland\'s CAIE provision is thin, so sittings may involve travel; Tallinn, two hours from Helsinki by ferry, is among the practical alternatives.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the Finland-Nairobi time difference?', a: 'It is the best alignment in our coverage: Finland runs EET (UTC+2) in winter — one hour behind Nairobi — and EEST (UTC+3) in summer, the same clock. Live teaching lands exactly inside the Finnish school day.' },
    { q: 'Which Finnish cities does Smartious cover?', a: 'Helsinki, Espoo, Tampere, Turku, Oulu, and Rovaniemi/Lapland have dedicated pages with local market context. Live online delivery works identically anywhere in the country — which in Lapland is precisely the point.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
  ctaSubhead: 'Two-gate admissions process. Submit the assessment request form — our Head of Admissions reviews every request within three business days. If accepted, the assessment fee is invoiced before the diagnostic is scheduled. If you want to talk through the notification, the examining-teacher relationship, or the 16-18 question first, ask — those conversations are better had before the school year than during it.',
